collect.summary:
Material collected on Wilhelm Xylander (Holtzman), 1532-1576, for a later article. The article itself is not written. Main sources read in full or at the relevant pages:
- the ADB article by Fr. Schöll (1898, pp. 582-593), full wikitext from Wikisource;
- Melchior Adam, Vitae Germanorum philosophorum (1615), pp. 289-291, in the CAMENA transcription;
- Adam, Apographum monumentorum Haidelbergensium (1612), p. 49, the epitaph;
- Gesner/Frisius, Bibliotheca (1583), pp. 315-316;
- Toepke, Matrikel der Universität Heidelberg II (1886);
- Scaligerana (1695);
- Sandys, vol. 2, p. 270;
- Cantor, Vorlesungen II (1892), pp. 504-509;
- Heath, Diophantus (2nd ed. 1910), pp. 17, 22-26, 48;
- the Basel online catalogue 'Griechischer Geist aus Basler Pressen' (F. Hieronymus), entries GG 98, 99, 101, 198, 247, 259, 268, 272, 286, 290, 296, 297, 298. These are long paraphrases of Xylander's dedications, with exact dates;
- swisscollections records (imprints, VD16 numbers, letters);
- Kalliope (letters);
- Axworthy, Galilaeana 2026;
- the Brepols and Meskens catalogue pages.

There is no NDB article. Drüll's Heidelberger Gelehrtenlexikon is not online, and its pages 562-563 were not read. Kühlmann et al. (2016) is the main modern documentary volume: it holds Xylander's paratexts. It was identified but not read.

Key corrections to the ADB:
- Cedrenus appeared in early 1566 (dedication dated 15 January 1566), not 1556.
- The Strasbourg clock Schediasma appeared in 1575 (and an item dated 1574), not 1675.
- The 1578 Basel Diodorus was edited by J. J. Grynaeus. Xylander's part in it that is confirmed is the Tryphiodorus. ADB's 'Latin Diodorus by Xylander' needs checking.
- The 'first vernacular Euclid' claim (ADB, Cantor) is wrong: Spanish and Italian translations came first.

Dates still in dispute: the logic chair (1562 or early 1563), the librarianship (1558 or 1561), and arrival in Basel (1556 per Adam; matriculation 20 July 1557).

For the 'marginal notes' theme, three points:
- Adam reports that Xylander wrote very little on paper.
- ADB reports that Xylander's own notes to the Psellus refer repeatedly to his German Euclid.
- Pal. gr. 391, with German marginal notes, may be his printer's copy for a Greek Diophantus.

No surviving books with his own marginalia were identified.

Twenty-one letters by Xylander and two to him were located; they are listed in the findings. The music section was not opened in any translation; only catalogue descriptions and the prefaces/dedications were used.

Files saved in raw/work (local, not in git):
- texts: adb_xylander_raw.txt, camena_adam_vitae_1.html, adam_vitae_phil_1615_djvu.txt, apogr_11064507bsb.txt, apogr_bub_gb_CHxy9sPSNbIC.txt, sandys_vol2_djvu.txt, heath_diophantus_1910_djvu.txt, cantor_vorl2_1892_djvu.txt, toepke2_1886.txt, gesner1583_djvu.txt, scal_bub_gb_mPcf6nFu0mUC.txt, axworthy_2026.pdf/.txt;
- GG entries: higg*.html;
- catalogue data: rec_*.html, swisscoll_search.html, kalliope_sru.xml, kalliope_sru_to.xml;
- also downloaded but unused: bursian1883_v1.txt, bursian1883_v1b.txt (Fraktur OCR unusable), bartsch1887_djvu.txt (no shelfmark found), and the remaining scal_*.txt Scaligerana editions.
